how did they segment
the data and analyze it,

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:10.140
and what did they
do that worked?

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:22.010
In a similar vein,
another user realized

00:28:22.010 --> 00:28:23.510
that we have a lot
of people sharing

00:28:23.510 --> 00:28:28.190
really helpful
tutorials for how to do

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:33.140
various analytics, machine
learning, and AI projects,

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:38.180
and work on Kaggle, and
created a set of topics,

00:28:38.180 --> 00:28:41.600
and then pulled out here's the
best kernels on Kaggle for each

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:43.700
of these topics, ranging
from linear regression,

00:28:43.700 --> 00:28:48.950
logistic regression, to
XGBoost, to deep learning,

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:52.300
and convolutional
neural networks,

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:57.290
to clustering algorithms,
through to a lot

00:28:57.290 --> 00:29:00.700
of other things that
don't get a lot of buzz,

00:29:00.700 --> 00:29:02.950
but are really important for
anyone applying analytics

00:29:02.950 --> 00:29:04.325
and machine learning
in practice,

00:29:04.325 --> 00:29:08.220
like doing high-quality
exploratory data analyses

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:10.440
so you understand what's
actually in the data set

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:11.481
that you're working with,

00:29:11.481 --> 00:29:14.720
and how to do feature
engineering, and components

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:18.857
along those lines.

00:29:18.857 --> 00:29:20.690
And again, one of the
cool things about this

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:24.425
is that this isn't
something that they

00:29:24.425 --> 00:29:26.300
had to go through and
manually pull together.

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:29.090
Instead, they were able
to create this analysis

00:29:29.090 --> 00:29:31.550
in our public data set
in a way that's actually

00:29:31.550 --> 00:29:34.220
produced and updated over time.

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:36.920
I'm going to go ahead and go
into the last bit of Kaggle

00:29:36.920 --> 00:29:41.840
for a little bit, then open back
up for questions at the end.

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:46.230
We heard from many
of our newer users

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:48.780
that they really
wanted more material

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:51.660
to get started in data
science and machine learning.

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:53.400
So about six months
ago, we launched

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:56.370
a new section of Kaggle
called Kaggle Learn that's

00:29:56.370 --> 00:30:00.850
run through kernels.

00:30:00.850 --> 00:30:07.480
And then this is a set of
about 10 different courses,

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:11.050
each of which consist of around
five to 10 different tutorials

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:13.610
and exercises that take
a week to go through,

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:17.950
covering areas like Python
for our analytics and machine

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:20.950
learning, or using SQL
as a data scientist,

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:25.690
or here's how to get started
with your first machine

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:28.090
learning models.

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:31.660
And our goal for
these is to teach you

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:34.090
just enough that you
can use to get started

00:30:34.090 --> 00:30:35.590
with your own machine
learning work,

00:30:35.590 --> 00:30:37.630
and then to get you
onto your own projects

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:38.830
as quickly as possible.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:40.780
Because we believe
that you learn the most

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:43.840
from starting to work, and
figuring out what works

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:46.480
and what doesn't on
your own projects,

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:49.810
versus spending a lot of time
following a very structured

00:30:49.810 --> 00:30:50.890
course and curriculum.

00:30:56.740 --> 00:31:00.580
So just as one example of
[INAUDIBLE] first starting

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:06.382
the first machine
learning project.

00:31:06.382 --> 00:31:09.030
We used a house
price prediction data

00:31:09.030 --> 00:31:10.830
set for this, where
based on things

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:16.020
like how many square
feet is in your house,

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:18.520
and components like that, how
many bedrooms do you have,

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:21.160
predict the sales
price of the home.

00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:30.530
And this is also something
we're considering

00:31:30.530 --> 00:31:33.040
how to take community
contributions from as well.

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:36.230
So if you're inspired to use
Kaggle as a teaching platform,

00:31:36.230 --> 00:31:39.604
then I'd love to speak
to you afterwards.

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:47.359
I'd encourage you, if there's
an area of analytics, or data

00:31:47.359 --> 00:31:48.900
science, or machine
learning that you

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:52.650
want to explore more of,
to check out Kaggle Learn,

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:55.380
and to get started
there to start

00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:56.720
applying it to your own work.

00:31:56.720 --> 00:32:00.370
